entry control-rods
term: Control Rods
image: ../../assets/images/terms/control rod.webp
related_terms: nuclear-reactor
related_terms: moderator
related_terms: defense-in-depth
related_terms: chain-reaction
related_terms: reactor-core
quick_facts: Made of neutron absorbers: boron, cadmium, hafnium
quick_facts: Inserted deeper → less power; withdrawn → more power
quick_facts: SCRAM: full insertion in seconds stops the chain reaction
quick_facts: Designed to fail safe — gravity drops them on power loss
quick_facts: Absorb neutrons without themselves becoming dangerously unstable

sources:
    {"organization":"NEA","title":"Nuclear Safety","url":"https://www.oecd-nea.org/jcms/pl_13561/nuclear-safety"}

definition:
    Neutron-absorbing rods — boron carbide, cadmium, silver-indium-cadmium, or hafnium — moved in and out of a reactor core to regulate the chain reaction. Inserted deeper, they soak up more neutrons and power falls; withdrawn, power rises. On an emergency shutdown (scram) they slam fully in within seconds, stopping the fission chain. Because they rely on gravity or hydraulics rather than electronics, they fail safe.

debunking_snippet:
    “Turning off a reactor is like switching off a light.” Control rods stop the fission chain almost instantly — but radioactive decay heat keeps the fuel hot for days, which is why cooling continues long after shutdown.
